MONGOCONFIG:-
Good question — this is a core Spring Boot concept 👍
You’re thinking in normal Java terms: *“No one is calling this method — so how is it executed?”*
But Spring works using **lifecycle hooks + dependency injection + annotations**.

Let’s break it simply.

---

# ✅ Why `checkMongoConnection()` Runs Automatically

Because of this annotation:

```
@PostConstruct
```

This tells Spring:

> “Run this method automatically **after the bean is created and dependencies are injected**.”

You never call it manually — **Spring calls it for you during application startup**.

---

# ✅ Step-by-Step What Happens When Server Starts

## 1️⃣ Spring Boot starts

Spring scans for classes with special annotations like:

```
@Configuration
@Component
@Service
@Repository
```

Your class has:

```
@Configuration
```

So Spring registers it as a **bean**.

---

## 2️⃣ Spring Creates the Bean

Spring sees this constructor:

```java
public MongoConnectionConfig(MongoClient mongoClient)
```

Spring says:

> “I need a MongoClient bean to create this object.”

Spring Boot auto-configures MongoDB and provides a `MongoClient` bean automatically (because of Spring Data MongoDB).

So it injects it.

---

## 3️⃣ Bean is Created → PostConstruct Runs

After:

* Object is created
* Dependencies injected

Spring looks for:

```
@PostConstruct
```

Then Spring automatically calls:

```
checkMongoConnection()
```

You never call it — **Spring lifecycle calls it**.

---

# ✅ Lifecycle Order

```
Spring starts
   ↓
Finds @Configuration class
   ↓
Creates MongoConnectionConfig bean
   ↓
Injects MongoClient
   ↓
Runs @PostConstruct method
   ↓
checkMongoConnection() executes
```

---

# ✅ Equivalent Without Spring (Normal Java)

In plain Java you’d have to do:

```java
MongoConnectionConfig config = new MongoConnectionConfig(client);
config.checkMongoConnection();   // manual call
```

Spring does this automatically for you.

---

# ✅ When is `@PostConstruct` Used?

Very common for:

* DB connection checks
* Cache warmup
* Loading configs
* Startup validation
* Logging environment info
* Preloading data

---

# ✅ Alternative Modern Way (Spring Boot Style)

You can also use:

## ✔️ ApplicationRunner

```java
@Component
public class StartupRunner implements ApplicationRunner {
    @Override
    public void run(ApplicationArguments args) {
        System.out.println("App started");
    }
}
```

---

## ✔️ CommandLineRunner

```java
@Bean
CommandLineRunner runner() {
    return args -> System.out.println("Started");
}
```

---

# ✅ Your Code is Correct — Nothing Missing

Your code runs because:

```
@Configuration  → bean created
@PostConstruct → auto executed
```

No manual call required.

